# vrbo-scraper — copy to .env next to the scripts and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   python3 env_config.py        # prints what was picked up, WITHOUT secrets
#
# Credentials belong here and never on a command line: a secret in argv is
# readable by anything that can run `ps` and lands in shell history.
# `.env` is in .gitignore; keep it that way.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#   an explicit flag  ->  an exported environment variable  ->  .env  ->  default
#
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Anything still carrying `{...}` braces
# is treated as unset, so a copied example is never sent to an API as if it
# were a key — the alternative is a 401 a long way from its cause.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2Captcha — one key, four separately billed products
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Captcha solving, the Scraping Browser API, proxies and fingerprints all sit
# behind this one key: https://2captcha.com
#
# Worth knowing before you buy any of it, because on this site the usual
# advice is wrong: the thing that decides whether Vrbo answers you is the
# BROWSER, not the address. Measured on the same residential exit seconds
# apart — Playwright's bundled Chromium got HTTP 429, real Chrome got HTTP
# 200 and the full grid. Install Chrome (`playwright install chrome`) before
# buying anything. What the paid products then buy is volume from many
# addresses, a specific exit country, and no browser to run yourself.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=your_2captcha_api_key_here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Scraping Browser API — a remote browser over CDP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   country-{cc}   picks the exit country. On this site that also picks the
#                  storefront's idea of where you are, which is worth
#                  matching to the hostname you are scraping
#   pid-{id}       a profile with persistent cookies, ONE live connection
#                  each — reuse pids rather than minting one per run
#
# --concurrency above 1 is refused on this site anyway (a Vrbo listing has no
# per-page address), so one run per pid is the shape here regardless.
#
# NOTE: selenium_scraper.py cannot use this. chromedriver's `debuggerAddress`
# takes a bare host:port with nowhere to put a password; Playwright and
# pyppeteer authenticate on the WebSocket upgrade and work fine.
VRBO_CDP_ENDPOINT=ws://{login}-zone-scraping_browser-country-us-pid-{profileId}:{password}@cb.2captcha.com:9222

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A proxy exit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read as a single exit, or use --proxy-file for a pool.
#
# What a proxy is and is NOT for on this site:
#
#  * It does NOT get a bundled Chromium past the front door. The refusal is a
#    property of the browser build, not the address — see the note above.
#
#  * It DOES spread the rate limit, which is the thing that actually stops a
#    multi-page run here. The data behind a page turn comes over POST
#    /graphql, and that endpoint is rate limited SEPARATELY from the HTML:
#    measured, the search page kept answering 200 while every /graphql POST
#    behind a next-press came back 429. --delay is the cheaper lever; a pool
#    is the one that scales.
#
# Note Selenium cannot authenticate a proxy at all (--proxy-server takes an
# address only). 2Captcha's IP-whitelist mode is the way around that: whitelist
# your address, ask /proxy/generate_white_list_connections for connections,
# and you get one `host:port` per exit with no credentials in them at all.
VRBO_PROXY=http://{user}:{password}@us.proxy.2captcha.com:2334

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A default URL, so a run needs no --url
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Either page kind, on any of the five measured storefronts:
#
#   search    https://www.vrbo.com/search?destination=Orlando,%20Florida,%20United%20States%20of%20America
#             https://www.fewo-direkt.de/search?destination=Berlin,%20Deutschland
#   property  https://www.vrbo.com/pdp/lo/63925107
#
# Add startDate and endDate to a search URL if you are monitoring prices.
# Without them the site quotes every property its OWN cheapest one-night
# stay, so the prices in one run are not comparable to each other and a row
# will appear to change price between runs when only the date moved.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: an output prefix. `--out` already carries a
# non-empty default, so the loader would never see it as unset and the
# variable would be silently inert — a setting that looks configurable and is
# not.
VRBO_URL=https://www.vrbo.com/search?destination=Orlando,%20Florida,%20United%20States%20of%20America
